{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792177919,"nonce":"AevCLt6jQv8+3XYV6CSuv4B5/FM=","title":"Lua: arithmetic on numbers of run-time kind clones both operand subtrees","message":"binary_vals passed a.clone(), b.clone() to number_binary whenever an operand is Ty::Number, in case number_binary declined the operator, so each `+`/`*` in a chain copied its whole left subtree (quadratic in the chain). number_binary's `number` closure also cloned `pre`, which holds the operands' let statements, and dropped the original.\n\nFound with a full-stack samply profile of nbody start-up (ZYLUA_PROFILE_STARTUP=40, warm-up off): typed-AST clone and drop were 523 of 3867 samples; number_binary and binary_vals owned about 180 of them.\n\nFix: decide up front whether the number path takes the operator (takes_number_path) and move the operands in; `number` takes `pre` by value. The lowered program is unchanged.\n\nMac, start-up instructions, ZYLUA_PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3 interleaved: nbody 2.404G -\u003e 2.263G (-5.9%), mandelbrot -0.9%, 17-benchmark total 11.771G -\u003e 11.613G (-1.3%); peak footprint unchanged. Official math, sort, bitwise neutral.\n\nChecked: Lua tests on Mac all pass (conformance 46/46).","files":null}]}